import Mathlib.RingTheory.Smooth.StandardSmooth

/-!
# Standard smooth presentations containing prescribed generators

Source: Stacks Project, Smoothing Ring Maps, Lemma Tag 07EY
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-standard-smooth-include-generators).
-/

universe u v

namespace Algebra

/-- A standard smooth algebra has a standard smooth presentation (a Mathlib
`SubmersivePresentation` on the variables `Fin (n + m)` with relations `Fin c`, whose
Jacobian variables are the first `c`, `P.map = Fin.castLE hc`) whose first `n` variables
map to any prescribed finite family, repetitions allowed. The presentation's
`span_range_relation_eq_ker` and `quotientEquiv` give the isomorphism
`R[x_1, …, x_{n+m}]/(f_1, …, f_c) ≃ₐ[R] A` sending `x_i` to `P.val i`, and
`jacobian_isUnit` with `jacobiMatrix_apply` says that the image of
`det (∂f_j/∂x_i)_{i,j ≤ c}` is a unit of `A`.
Source: Stacks Project, Smoothing Ring Maps, Lemma Tag 07EY
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-standard-smooth-include-generators).

Proof (the eight steps of the DAG node):
1. By `def-weil-pbc-standard-smooth-algebra`, take a presentation of `A` with `m` variables
   and `d ≤ m` relations, whose initial Jacobian determinant is a unit. The polynomial map
   to `A` is surjective with kernel generated by the relations.
2. Lift each prescribed element to a polynomial `hᵢ` in those variables. Repetitions impose
   no restriction on these choices.
3. Adjoin `n` variables `xᵢ` mapping to the prescribed elements, using the universal property
   in `def-weil-pbc-mv-polynomial-ring`. The resulting map remains surjective and kills
   `xᵢ - hᵢ` and the old relations.
4. Its kernel is generated by these relations: substitution `xᵢ ↦ hᵢ` changes any polynomial
   by an element of the ideal generated by `xᵢ - hᵢ`. For a polynomial in the kernel, its
   substituted polynomial belongs to the old relation ideal. This proves both inclusions.
5. The quotient map induces the required algebra isomorphism. Put `c = n + d`, order the
   new relations first, and obtain `n ≤ c ≤ n + m`.
6. By `def-weil-pbc-polynomial-partial-derivative`, the initial Jacobian matrix has identity
   upper left block, zero upper right block, lower left block given by derivatives of `-hᵢ`,
   and lower right block equal to the old initial Jacobian matrix.
7. By `lem-weil-pbc-block-triangular-determinant`, its determinant is the old determinant,
   whose image is a unit in `A`.
8. The quotient isomorphism sends the first `n` variable classes to the prescribed family;
   together with the unit determinant and bounds, it is the desired presentation. -/
theorem exists_standardSmoothPresentation_include_generators
    {R : Type u} {A : Type v} [CommRing R] [CommRing A] [Algebra R A]
    [IsStandardSmooth R A] (n : ℕ) (a : Fin n → A) :
    ∃ (m c : ℕ) (hc : c ≤ n + m) (P : SubmersivePresentation R A (Fin (n + m)) (Fin c)),
      n ≤ c ∧ P.map = Fin.castLE hc ∧ ∀ i : Fin n, P.val (Fin.castAdd m i) = a i := by
  sorry

end Algebra
